problems.problem:
Let $ABCDEF$ be a regular hexagon with centre $O$. Let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of the sides $\overline{CD}$ and $\overline{DE}$, and let $L$ be the intersection point of the lines $AM$ and $BN$. Prove:
(a) $P(ABL) = P(DMLN)$ (where $P$ denotes area);
(b) $\angle ALD = \angle OLN = 60^\circ$;
(c) $\angle OLD = 90^\circ$.

A quadrilateral $ABCD$ with angles $\alpha = 60^\circ$, $\beta = 90^\circ$, $\gamma = 120^\circ$ is given. The diagonals $\overline{AC}$ and $\overline{BD}$ intersect at the point $S$, where $2|BS| = |SD| = 2d$. From the midpoint $P$ of the diagonal $\overline{AC}$ the perpendicular $\overline{PM}$ is dropped onto the diagonal $\overline{BD}$, and from the point $S$ the perpendicular $\overline{SN}$ onto $\overline{PB}$.
Prove:
(a) $|MS| = |NS| = \dfrac{d}{2}$;
(b) $|AD| = |DC|$;
(c) $P(ABCD) = \dfrac{9d^2}{2}$, where $P(ABCD)$ denotes the area of $ABCD$.
